course: biology
lesson: 338
title: The vegetable patch: peas, potatoes, tomatoes and slugs
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A vegetable patch is a place where you eat different parts of plants: the seeds of a pea, the swollen underground stem of a potato, the fruit of a tomato and the root of a radish.
explain: Watch a pea plant for a day: its thin curly tendrils feel for a stick and wrap round it within hours, hauling the plant up towards the light like a climber on a rope.
explain: At night the slugs come out, gliding on their slime and nibbling holes in the lettuce, while a bumblebee hums loudly on a tomato flower in the day, shaking the pollen out like salt from a salt cellar.
explain: In cartoons potatoes grow on trees and carrots wink from bushes. Real plants hide their stores underground, and some close relatives, like potatoes and tomatoes, are good to eat in one part and poisonous in another.
fact: Pea tendrils feel touch and curl around whatever they meet, sometimes within hours, so peas can climb towards the light without growing a thick stem.
fact: A pea pod often holds six to nine peas.
fact: A monk called Gregor Mendel grew thousands of pea plants to learn how features pass from parents to their young, and his work started the science of genetics.
fact: Potatoes are swollen underground stems that store food, and the eyes on a potato are buds that can grow into new plants.
fact: Potatoes that turn green in the light have a bitter poison, and the green part must not be eaten, so potatoes are kept in the dark.
fact: Potato plants grow small green berries that look like little tomatoes but are poisonous; potatoes and tomatoes are close relatives, and only the potato underground is eaten.
fact: Scientists call a tomato a fruit because it holds seeds, it turns red as it ripens by making a red colour called lycopene, and tomatoes first came from South America.
fact: Bumblebees shake tomato flowers to release their pollen, which is called buzz pollination, and greenhouse growers keep boxes of bumblebees to pollinate tomatoes.
fact: Slugs feed at night when it is damp, and hedgehogs, thrushes, frogs and beetles eat them.
fact: Slug pellets can harm hedgehogs and birds, and the most harmful kind was banned in Britain.
fact: A slug has four tentacles: the top two carry eyes and the bottom two feel and smell; it can pull them in if touched, and its slime trail helps it slide and find its way home.
fact: Worms, fungi and bacteria break kitchen scraps down into rich compost, the middle of the heap can get warm, and the compost feeds the patch next year.
fact: Radishes can be ready in about four weeks, so they suit a first garden, and the part we eat is a swollen root.
exercise: What do pea tendrils do? | Feel touch and curl around whatever they meet | Catch small flies and other insects for the plant to eat | Suck water from the air | Grow into new pea pods
exercise: Why do peas climb instead of growing a thick stem? | Climbing lifts them to the light cheaply | Climbing keeps the hungry slugs off the pods | Thick stems are poisonous to peas | Peas cannot make wood at all
exercise: About how many peas are usually in a pod? | Six to nine | Only one or two | About fifty peas | Exactly twelve
exercise: What did Gregor Mendel learn by growing thousands of pea plants? | How features pass from parents to their young | How to keep slugs and birds away from his pea plants | How to make peas grow on trees | Why peas are green
exercise: What is a potato? | A swollen underground stem that stores food | A fat root that drinks water from the soil | The seed of the potato flower | A fruit with seeds inside
exercise: What are the eyes on a potato? | Buds that can grow into new plants | Marks left by the farmer's fork when digging | Holes made by worms | Seeds of the potato
exercise: Why must green parts of a potato not be eaten? | They hold a bitter poison | They are not ripe enough yet | They are much too hard to chew | They taste just like old grass
exercise: Why are potatoes kept in the dark? | Light makes them turn green and poisonous | Dark makes them grow bigger and sweeter while they wait | Light makes them melt | Dark keeps them warm
exercise: What are the small green berries on a potato plant? | Poisonous fruits that look like little tomatoes | Baby potatoes that will drop into the soil | Sweet berries to pick for pudding | Eggs laid by beetles
exercise: Why do scientists call a tomato a fruit? | It holds seeds | It is sweet and juicy | It is bright red when ripe | It grows above the ground
exercise: Where did tomatoes first come from? | South America | Italy, where they grow everywhere | China | Ireland
exercise: What does a bumblebee do on a tomato flower? | Shakes it by buzzing so the pollen comes out | Drinks all the sweet water from the middle of it | Bites the flower open | Lays its eggs in it
exercise: When do slugs eat the lettuce? | At night when it is damp | At midday in hot sun | Only in winter | Only when it snows
exercise: Which animals eat slugs? | Hedgehogs, thrushes, frogs and beetles | Bees, butterflies and other flying insects | Cows, horses and sheep | Only other slugs
exercise: How many tentacles does a slug have? | Four | Only two | Six or seven | Eight
exercise: What do a slug's top two tentacles carry? | Its eyes | Its teeth | Its tiny ears | Its babies
exercise: What turns kitchen peelings into compost? | Worms, fungi and bacteria | The heat of the summer sun alone | Garden slugs and snails only | Rain slowly washing them away
exercise: Which part of a radish do we eat? | A swollen root | A seed pod | A flower | A stem with leaves
test: «just chuck slug pellets everywhere, kill the lot of 'em» What is the problem? | Pellets can harm hedgehogs and birds that eat slugs | Pellets make lettuce grow too fast | Slugs love pellets and get bigger | There is no problem at all
test: How could you protect lettuce without harming hedgehogs? | Welcome slug eaters like frogs and thrushes, and pick slugs off at night | Cover the lettuce and the soil around it with a thick layer of salt every day | Spray the hedgehogs | Plant the lettuce in the pond
test: «it's a vegetable, you don't put tomato in a fruit salad, you muppet» What is right? | Scientists call it a fruit because it holds seeds, though cooks treat it as a vegetable | It is a vegetable because it is eaten with salt, and fruit is always sweet | It is only a vegetable | It is a root
test: «potato plant's got mini tomatoes on it, sick, let's eat them» What is right? | They are poisonous potato berries; do not eat them | They are real tomatoes, carried over from a tomato plant by bees | They are sweet baby potatoes | They are safe once washed
test: In a cartoon a farmer picks potatoes off a tall tree. What is right? | Potatoes are swollen stems that grow underground | Potatoes grow on trees in hot countries | Potatoes grow inside pea pods | Potatoes fall from the sky in rain
test: «this spud's gone a bit green, whatever, chips are chips» What should be done? | Cut off and throw away the green part, which has a bitter poison | Eat it anyway, because frying it in hot oil makes the green part safe | Paint it white first | Give it to the dog instead
test: Why does a compost heap get warm in the middle? | Billions of tiny living things breaking it down give off heat | The sun shines down through the heap and warms the middle most | Rain soaks in and turns warm in the middle | Worms rub together to make fire
test: Why might radishes be good for a child's first garden? | They grow fast and can be ready in about four weeks | They grow bigger than pumpkins, so they are easy to find | They grow only in winter | They need no water ever
test: Why does a slug follow its own trail? | Its slime trail helps it find its way home | It wants to clean up its mess | It is hunting itself | It cannot turn round
test: Count them: 3 slugs climb onto a lettuce. How many tentacles and how many eyes are there? | 12 tentacles and 6 eyes | 6 tentacles and 6 eyes | 12 tentacles and 12 eyes | 8 tentacles and 3 eyes
test: Count them: a hedgehog and a frog hunt slugs in the patch. How many legs are hunting? | 8 | 4 | 6 | 10
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the edible part of the potato plant? | It is a swollen underground stem in which food is stored | It is the fruit borne above the ground | It is the principal root of the plant | It is a seed enclosed within a pod
exam: Identify the principal reason for which potatoes ought to be stored in the absence of light. | Exposure to light causes the formation of a bitter poison, visible as a green colour | Exposure to light causes the potatoes to liquefy | Darkness increases the size of stored potatoes | Darkness prevents the potatoes from being consumed by bees
exam: Ascertain the contribution of Gregor Mendel to the biological sciences. | Through the cultivation of thousands of pea plants he established the founding principles of genetics | He was the first to bring the potato across the ocean from South America and grow it in European fields | He discovered the red colour of the tomato | He invented the first compost heap
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the hazards of slug pellets. | Certain slug pellets may harm hedgehogs and birds, and the most harmful kind has been prohibited in Britain | Slug pellets are harmless to every creature other than slugs | Slug pellets are beneficial to the health of hedgehogs | Slug pellets are required by law in every vegetable garden
